#{[17592186387554 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}{(\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}+2\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387561 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ((\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}t(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)+t\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =t({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387629 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=y,R={z}^{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [17592186387685 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/9e/9eceb8ab173995a8325fb1a8452baaa7f1071e569391e9a49b69c047d76f3b52.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387699 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d8ee02b2b7282349604dc6305c0b2be796cfdbd2937e06d089e660683a5785ad.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387706 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}y}+2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c4/c40d9fdfd10b3be4153c9c8ea3a6302af3cf0392533767313c467bdf7dcec1d7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387754 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=4,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387803 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387859 "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-1)^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}2(t-1)⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}-2,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-2⟩+⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}-2,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-2,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387866 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)-2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{t-1}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(1)-2\\mathbf{g}(1))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(1)-2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(1) \\\\  & =⟨2(1),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{1-1}⟩-2⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3(1)^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩-2⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩-⟨2,6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387880 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})=-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {t}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -{t}^{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}-{t}^{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{-t}(3-t) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387901 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\sqrt{u})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})=-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t))\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u(t)}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{-t}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{t/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2{e}^{-2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387908 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}(u+1)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u+1},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(u(t))^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u(t)+1},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{t}{1+t},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387971 "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3} & t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}⟩, \\\\ \\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ t & 5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}t & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}t & 5 \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t) & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}⟩+⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{3}-10t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}-2t,\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388083 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{(}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ (-\\mathbf{i}-4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ -1-4x & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & =y+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8}).$"] [17592186388904 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2-\\frac{t}{2}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,2-\\frac{t}{2}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{t}{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={t}^{3}-2(2-\\frac{t}{2})={t}^{3}+t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388911 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2{e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,2{e}^{t}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388932 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388981 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-x\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ne 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}x\\ne 2\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $x=2$ and shade *right* and *left* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/23/2371c9724d66e0f7d74c45e173f9625a986615185c10d55c029976baa82d0877.png)"] [17592186388988 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-2x>0\\,⟹\\,y>2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y>2x\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $>$ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $y=2x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/42/42e2faf3df63b038b57d2fddc9b7ef2c2e720dae6e0a8524d602b7ac93681b3d.png)"] [17592186388995 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}(x.y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/70/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)"] [17592186389119 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\operatorname{ln}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}({r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}({r}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389139 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=2-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-x \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/58/587c299c1bdf89061be205cf185d6335fba865d2e30c81cac5b9d223fc84b486.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ),\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389181 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)"] [17592186389188 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"] [17592186389195 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =2u+2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+2v & <0 \\\\ 2v & <-2u \\\\ v & <-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png)"] [17592186389202 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ -v & -u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-u)-(-v)\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & >0 \\\\ v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(2,3)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png)"] [17592186389209 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)"] [17592186389319 "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y) & =P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389326 "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =15x{y}^{2}-x, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)=5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y) & =P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(15x{y}^{2}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389339 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2x\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=2x$ and $Q(x,y)=-2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=2xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=2xy$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2xy & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=0$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)"] [17592186389346 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)"] [17592186389511 "Recall that the hyperbola\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18\\,⇒\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{sec}^{2}⁡t}{9}-\\frac{2{tan}^{2}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}t},\\hspace{0.167em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}t}.$"] [17592186389518 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}12{x}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}12{x}^{2}}.$"] [17592186389525 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}8{z}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}8{z}^{2}}.$"] [17592186389532 "Recall that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $z\\ge 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡t-4{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{tan}t},\\hspace{0.167em}y,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\operatorname{sec}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{tan}t}.$"] [17592186389539 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{36} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{sin}t}.$"] [17592186389553 "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{cos}^{2}⁡t}{25}+\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡t}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{sin}t}.$"] [17592186390360 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-15)\\mathbf{i}-(3-6)\\mathbf{j}+(5-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-15,3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390737 "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}6t,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 12t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}12t & 0 \\\\ 3 & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}12t & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}12t-48{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}-6⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & 2t & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6t & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 6 \\\\ 6t & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}6{t}^{2} & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}6{t}^{2} & 2t \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-28t,\\hspace{0.222em}-24{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t) & =⟨8t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}12t-48{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}-6⟩+⟨-28t,\\hspace{0.222em}-24{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-20t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\hspace{0.222em}72{t}^{3}-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390798 "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\times \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\times \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-6t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\times \\mathbf{f}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -6t \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2t & -1 \\\\ -2t & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2t & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\times \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t) & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩+⟨0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186391631 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t\\operatorname{cos}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}t\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+2t\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+{t}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{t}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-2t\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L &={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392884 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392891 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2})^{2}+(3)^{2}+(4{t}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+9+16{t}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{16{t}^{6}+9{t}^{4}+9}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393001 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}2t \\\\ {e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393008 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{cos}u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 3{u}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}(u(t)) \\\\ 3(u(t))^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u(t)}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t}) \\\\ 3({e}^{t})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t}) \\\\ 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t}){e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393105 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{5t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5t{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(3t+1){e}^{3t} \\\\ (4t+1){e}^{4t} \\\\ (5t+1){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393112 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-7)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{t}^{3}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-7){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393618 "Since $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+1,$ $y={t}^{2}-1,$ and $z={t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =({t}^{2}+1)+({t}^{2}-1)-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393625 "Since $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393632 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2{t}^{2}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+16{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+16{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{1+16{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393639 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t).$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}(t)⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 2e\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(t))\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}t}=2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}2{t}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394497 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394504 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $$\n\\displaystyle {(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2d/2d50f2375c071d8bfb4c5c58a836bef76a70557c2593d4df9acf48f9f0e7527b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/98/98e9c8442c17cbc77083c5dc55a9d74324befa05e3cd47e1c65c38cd510c08c6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394545 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/32/32c4bf490df51121ddf1575df8211033185d392fd5b08519971723b820da191f.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/b1/b1e2eac0041d0b0cb802561b3fb83fd8fab1b940cb6c42f698879837c06cbff0.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/19/193601785ff94605d12b91b7cab1e7390af532ba1912530bd8d3f492324e2788.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394552 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/bf/bf84eed655256e63439ca907610c4b0b022c6c480d5ab0b6c12d6826af9f0907.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/6d/6de2b6e2b9925577f8cf0961f8466707f770ddca175a04dac9b592542aac16e8.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/4a/4a56d4023b03b40b4c1be5f22809963d6c745fd90ed3d860cf69d555b3e869ae.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\operatorname{ln}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }r\\operatorname{ln}({r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }r\\operatorname{ln}({r}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394654 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}3\\le r\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394661 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ and $(\\sqrt{3},-3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/59/5991463a56d8358164d32b15f73e938573463d8d706d2b9c8fc06803326a5f0f.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395845 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 2(y-3) \\\\  & =4(y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4(y-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4(y-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} &=0 \\\\ 4(y-3) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point $(0,3).$"] [17592186397957 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}3\\sqrt{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186397964 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}6u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 6u & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}6u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6u \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-6u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6u)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(-6u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{u}^{2}+4+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72{u}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(18{u}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186397971 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(-2{u}^{2})^{2}+(4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398228 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2-3t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\color{blue}4-9t})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398235 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x>{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x>{y}^{2}\\}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186398361 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$"] [17592186398507 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨3x,\\hspace{0.222em}3y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x=u\\operatorname{cos}v\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}v\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩ \\\\  & =3u\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v)+3u\\operatorname{sin}v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v)+{u}^{2}\\cdot u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v)+{u}^{3} \\\\  & =-5{u}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-5{u}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398514 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=2u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)\\cdot (-2)+(2u-v)\\cdot (2)+(3u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u-2v+4u-2v+3u-2v \\\\  & =5u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u-6v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({\\color{blue}5u-6v})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398521 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨zy,\\hspace{0.222em}xz,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)v,\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)u,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)v,\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)u,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)v\\cdot (-1)+(u-v)u\\cdot (1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-uv+{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}-uv+uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨zy,\\hspace{0.222em}xz,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398534 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨xz,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y=uv\\,z=v} \\\\  & =⟨uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\cdot ⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩ \\\\  & =uv\\cdot v+uv\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot u \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398562 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}+y+2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398569 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xz) \\\\  & =2x-z+4x \\\\  & =6x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(6x-z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6x-z}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186398947 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$"] [17592186398954 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$"] [17592186400121 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-1 \\\\ -1 & \\le -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1-1 & \\le 1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1+1 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+2 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\le 1+2 \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$"] [17592186400338 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{k}^{2} & 7 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(7\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)-7(6\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 4)+4(6\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}+0-84 \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}-84 \\\\  & =14({k}^{2}-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 14({k}^{2}-6) & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =6 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\sqrt{6}$ or $k=\\sqrt{6}.$"] [17592186400373 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)\\mathbf{i}-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})\\mathbf{j}=0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 &=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"] [17592186400450 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"] [17592186400750 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-6))\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-6))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(10-0)\\mathbf{i}-(8-6)\\mathbf{j}+(0-(-30))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =10\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+30\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186401226 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}y(-x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186401233 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(-2y)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
